## 2. Gate — launch has no test

Owner command as written (`grep test -e cli/cmd_pm_launch.oo`) is invalid
grep usage: `-e` takes the next token as a second pattern, so grep looks
for a file named `test` and exits 2. That is not a source hit.

Intended emptiness holds:

    grep -n test cli/cmd_pm_launch.oo
    grep -nF 'test -e' cli/cmd_pm_launch.oo

Both empty. grep exit 1.

`--exec` after an ok gate:

    rargs = list_push(rargs, path);
    run_oodac_run(fs, sys, rargs);

One `run_oodac_run` at line 117. No `sys_exec`. No `sh -c`. No `test -e`.

Gate runs first. `pm_launch_gate` is line 96. Fail calls `launch_die`
(`process_exit(2)`) before line 117. `cli/pm_launch.oo` still returns
`err yank`, `err sha256 mismatch`, `err caps`. Those strings are unchanged.

`run_oodac_run` finds oodac and starts `oodac run` plus the quoted path.
The `.oo` bytes are not a `sh` script.

## 3. Gate — complete has no process_exec

`textDocument/completion` is in-process:

    return lsp_ok(id, lsp_complete_result(fs, body));

`lsp_complete_result` takes `&FsCap` only. It reads a `file://` path.
It filters a fixed catalog. It does not take ProcessCap. It does not name oodac.

Proof of no call:

    grep -n 'process_exec(' cli/lsp_complete.oo
    grep -n 'sys_exec(' cli/lsp_complete.oo
    grep -n oodac cli/lsp_complete.oo

All empty.

One comment at line 52: `Do not call process_exec`. That is not a call.
`cli/lsp_reply.oo` has no `process_exec` and no `sys_exec`.

URI `..` does not walk to `/etc`. Load order is extract, then `..`,
then exists, then `read_file`.

    if str_index_of(p, "..") >= 0 { return ""; }

`file://../etc/passwd` and `file://foo/../../etc/passwd` never call `read_file`.

Token: FsCap is enough. No new SysCap on the complete path.

## 4. Gate — bench uses now_ms, not date

Main signature (RULES 1.4 record):

    pub fn main(fs: &FsCap, sys: &SysCap, time: &TimeCap, args: List[String])

Bench signature:

    pub fn run_ooda_bench(fs: &FsCap, sys: &SysCap, time: &TimeCap, args: List[String])

`time` goes only to `run_ooda_bench`. Other commands keep FsCap and SysCap.

Clock on disk (`cli/cmd_more.oo`):

    let t0: Int = now_ms(time);
    sys_exec(sys, "sh", "-c", prefix + " check " + sq(path));
    let t1: Int = now_ms(time);

Optional `--run` uses `now_ms(time)` again. Negative delta becomes 0.

Proof:

    grep -nE 'now_ms|date |date\+|EPOCH|SECONDS|sleep_ms' cli/cmd_more.oo

Hits: four `now_ms(time)` at lines 117, 119, 130, 132. No `date`.
No bash timer. No `EPOCH`. No `SECONDS`. No `sleep_ms`.

SysCap starts oodac through `find_oodac` + `sq`. It is not the clock.
The user file is one quoted operand. It is not `sh PATH`.

